call_details.response:
Reasoning:
1.  There is no immediate four-in-a-row available for either side, so the move must be chosen for strategic value and to avoid giving “x” an instant win on the reply.
2.  All columns (0-6) are legally playable.  The lowest free slots are  
   0→r5, 1→r5, 2→r4, 3→r2, 4→r4, 5→r5, 6→r5.
3.  “x” cannot win on its next move from the present position (it never has three contiguous pieces with an empty fourth square).
4.  Among our options:
     • playing at 5 (row 5, col 5) only blocks a possible future horizontal line for x, but yields no strong threat for us;  
     • playing at 2 (row 4, col 2) gives us two vertically but lets x drop immediately at row 3, col 2 to block;  
     • plays in the outer columns (0,1,6) are least valuable and create no threat;  
     • playing at 4 (row 4, col 4) sets up a diagonal, but x can answer at row 5, col 5 to break it.
5.  Dropping in column 3 (row 2) is safest and strongest:
     • it seizes the central column — the most valuable file in Connect-4;  
     • it stacks a second “o” directly on top of our existing disc, giving us two in a column with two more still open above (a potential future vertical win);  
     • it does not create any new four-in-a-row for x on the reply; after this move x still has no column that wins immediately.
6.  Therefore column 3 maximises our long-term winning chances while keeping the position completely safe in the short term.

Final Answer: 3

